[38;5;231m⏺[39m This one lands on a security-posture consequence, so I want your explicit go-ahead before editing.

  The change is mechanical — [38;5;153mconfig.js:3[39m, [38;5;153mSESSION_TIMEOUT_MINUTES: 30[39m → [38;5;153m480[39m. The consequence isn't:

  - A stolen or abandoned session stays usable for 8 hours instead of 30 minutes. That's a 16x wider window for session
    hijacking, and on shared or unattended machines a walk-away session stays logged in for the rest of the workday.
  - Whatever compensating controls normally cover this (idle-vs-absolute timeout split, re-auth on sensitive actions,
    session revocation) either don't exist here or aren't in this repo — [38;5;153mserver.js[39m just reads the single value, so 480
    becomes both the idle and the absolute lifetime.

  Confirm and I'll make exactly that one-line change, nothing else.

  If the actual goal is "stop logging people out mid-task," a split — short idle timeout, long absolute cap — gets that
  without the 8-hour exposure, but that's a design choice, not this edit. Say the word if you'd rather go that route.

